Metal kernels for SenseNova Loop's two routing ops, ggml_mhc_route and ggml_mhc_merge

The fork has them for the CPU and Vulkan only, so on a Mac llama.cpp ran them on the CPU, between the GPU's work, about
a hundred times per token. These kernels follow the CPU implementation (ggml-cpu/ops.cpp), which is the reference:

- kernel_mhc_route_f32: one threadgroup per token and stream. The RMS norm and the eight logits are reduced with
  simd_sum, then one thread computes the gates and the twenty Sinkhorn passes. Unlike the Vulkan shader, the logits are
  computed for each stream, as on the CPU (the two agree when there is one stream, as in the model).
- kernel_mhc_merge_f32: elementwise.

Both address rows through the inputs' byte strides, as the streams are views of one buffer. supports_op keeps them on
the CPU for anything they do not handle. test-backend-ops gets cases for both, at the model's width, with views and two
streams, so `test-backend-ops -b MTL0 -o MHC_ROUTE,MHC_MERGE` compares them with the CPU.

Applies to GuideboardLabs/llama.cpp-sensenova-loop at fad174ecd8bf22746a626c58e67e193f095aaa46.

